{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794760662093,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd5ffeS4J0KdMtC6TlEK","part":{"partID":"prt_1e0125ac5001459bVqFf6x64kn","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd5ffeS4J0KdMtC6TlEK","messageID":"msg_1e0124d80001Av7zN3Q7EvfRXj","type":"tool","id":"call_45d72df409234beda6981960","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/hooks/shared/hook-flags.cjs"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/hooks/shared/hook-flags.cjs, lines 1-350\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Hook Kill-Switch Resolver\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: \"use strict\";\n5: \n6: // Central kill-switch resolver for every repo-authored runtime hook. A hook is\n7: // enabled by default and only goes silent when the operator sets a kill-switch,\n8: // so adding this guard changes no behavior until a flag is set. The few concerns\n9: // in DEFAULT_OFF invert that and stay silent until their enable switch is set,\n10: // though a kill-switch still silences them. The master\n11: // switch disables the whole enforcement layer at once; a per-concern switch\n12: // disables one hook family across every runtime that carries it. Names that\n13: // predate this convention are honored as aliases so existing operator config\n14: // keeps working.\n15: //\n16: // Flags come from two places: the live environment, and an optional operator\n17: // config file (hook-flags.env) that lets a user persist their choices without\n18: // exporting env vars every session. The environment always wins over the file,\n19: // so a persistent default in the file can still be overridden per session.\n20: \n21: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n22: // 1. IMPORTS\n23: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n24: \n25: const fs = require(\"node:fs\");\n26: const path = require(\"node:path\");\n27: \n28: // Bridge legacy env names onto their new equivalents once, as early as possible.\n29: // Nearly every runtime hook loads this module, so wiring the bridge here covers\n30: // the whole library from a single place; entry points with no flag dependency\n31: // (the standalone daemon launchers) call it themselves.\n32: require(\"./env-aliases.cjs\").applyEnvAliases();\n33: \n34: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n35: // 2. CONSTANTS\n36: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n37: \n38: const MASTER_FLAG = \"SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED\";\n39: // The master switch was renamed from its opaque prefix to a self-describing one.\n40: // The old name stays honored so any operator who exported it keeps their opt-out.\n41: const MASTER_ALIASES = [\"MK_HOOKS_DISABLED\"];\n42: \n43: // Canonical per-concern kill-switch, where the new name does not follow the\n44: // default `SYSTEM_<CONCERN>_DISABLED` shape because the hook is owned by a named\n45: // skill or CLI surface rather than the framework core. Concerns not listed here\n46: // fall back to the default shape in concernFlag().\n47: const CONCERN_CANONICAL = {\n48:   goal: \"OPENCODE_GOAL_DISABLED\",\n49:   dispatch: \"CLI_DISPATCH_AUDIT_DISABLED\",\n50:   \"mcp-route-guard\": \"MCP_ROUTE_GUARD_DISABLED\",\n51:   \"codex-watchdog\": \"CODEX_HOOKS_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\",\n52:   \"git-preflight\": \"SK_GIT_PREFLIGHT_DISABLED\",\n53:   \"post-edit-quality\": \"SK_CODE_POST_EDIT_QUALITY_DISABLED\",\n54: };\n55: \n56: // concern -> extra env vars that also disable it. Three generations coexist here\n57: // so operator config written against any of them keeps working: the still-older\n58: // MK_/SPECKIT_ aliases, and the self-describing variant names that a concern's\n59: // own plugin/config/docs use where they differ from concernFlag()'s canonical\n60: // (e.g. a plugin's *_PLUGIN_DISABLED / *_HOOK_DISABLED const, or a doc's shorter\n61: // spelling). concernFlag() supplies the one canonical name; everything a real\n62: // surface also documents or exports for the same concern is listed here so the\n63: // documented switch actually disables the hook.\n64: const LEGACY_ALIASES = {\n65:   goal: [\"OPENCODE_GOAL_PLUGIN_DISABLED\", \"MK_GOAL_DISABLED\", \"MK_GOAL_PLUGIN_DISABLED\"],\n66:   dispatch: [\"SYSTEM_DISPATCH_DISABLED\", \"MK_DISPATCH_DISABLED\", \"MK_CLI_DISPATCH_AUDIT_DISABLED\"],\n67:   // The preflight guard split off from the audit log, but the whole-surface\n68:   // switch that predates the split still has to silence both halves.\n69:   \"dispatch-guard\": [\"SYSTEM_DISPATCH_DISABLED\", \"MK_DISPATCH_DISABLED\"],\n70:   \"skill-advisor\": [\n71:     \"SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED\",\n72:     \"SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED\",\n73:     \"MK_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED\",\n74:     \"MK_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED\",\n75:     \"MK_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED\",\n76:     \"SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED\",\n77:     \"SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED\",\n78:   ],\n79:   completion: [\n80:     \"SYSTEM_SPECKIT_COMPLETION_DISABLED\",\n81:     \"SYSTEM_COMPLETION_SENTINEL_DISABLED\",\n82:     \"MK_COMPLETION_DISABLED\",\n83:     \"MK_COMPLETION_SENTINEL_DISABLED\",\n84:     \"MK_SPECKIT_COMPLETION_DISABLED\",\n85:   ],\n86:   \"spec-gate\": [\"MK_SPEC_GATE_DISABLED\", \"SPECKIT_SPEC_GATE_DISABLED\"],\n87:   \"mcp-route-guard\": [\"MK_MCP_ROUTE_GUARD_DISABLED\"],\n88:   \"codex-watchdog\": [\"CODEX_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\", \"MK_CODEX_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\", \"MK_CODEX_HOOKS_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\"],\n89:   \"dist-freshness\": [\"MK_DIST_FRESHNESS_DISABLED\", \"MK_DIST_FRESHNESS_GUARD_DISABLED\"],\n90:   \"git-preflight\": [\"MK_GIT_PREFLIGHT_DISABLED\"],\n91:   \"hook-install\": [\"MK_HOOK_INSTALL_DISABLED\"],\n92:   \"permission-policy\": [\"MK_PERMISSION_POLICY_DISABLED\"],\n93:   \"session-lifecycle\": [\"MK_SESSION_LIFECYCLE_DISABLED\"],\n94:   \"session-cleanup\": [\"MK_SESSION_CLEANUP_DISABLED\"],\n95:   \"task-dispatch\": [\"MK_TASK_DISPATCH_DISABLED\"],\n96:   \"post-edit-quality\": [\"MK_POST_EDIT_QUALITY_DISABLED\"],\n97: };\n98: \n99: // concern -> the switch that turns it on. A concern listed here is off until the\n100: // operator opts in, because it acts on the session itself (the relaunch loop can\n101: // end a running session and start a new one), except on a runtime DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES\n102: // names for it. The opt-in resolves like every other flag, environment over file,\n103: // and the master and kill switches still win over it.\n104: const DEFAULT_OFF = {\n105:   \"session-handover\": \"SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_ENABLED\",\n106: };\n107: \n108: // The CLI runtimes that can carry a default-off concern. An operator may want the\n109: // relaunch loop on in one runtime and off in another, so each runtime gets its own\n110: // enable switch, SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_<RUNTIME>_ENABLED, which answers before\n111: // the global one whenever it holds a value. A caller names its runtime explicitly;\n112: // the resolver never guesses it from the environment.\n113: const RUNTIMES = Object.freeze([\"claude\", \"codex\", \"pi\", \"opencode\", \"cursor\", \"devin\", \"vibe\"]);\n114: \n115: // concern -> the runtimes where a default-off concern is on when neither its runtime\n116: // switch nor its global switch holds a value. Claude Code's hand-over needs no extra\n117: // wiring beyond the hooks every install registers, so a project gets it without\n118: // finding the switch; the other runtimes still wait for their owner to opt in. A set\n119: // value, the master switch and the kill switches all answer before this default.\n120: const DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES = Object.freeze({\n121:   \"session-handover\": Object.freeze([\"claude\"]),\n122: });\n123: \n124: // Main switch prefix -> the second spelling also read for it. People call the hand-over\n125: // \"in-session handover\" too, so a switch written with that name works. The second\n126: // spelling answers only when the main switch is unset or blank, so the main name wins\n127: // whenever both hold a value.\n128: const SWITCH_ALIAS_PREFIXES = Object.freeze({\n129:   SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_: \"SYSTEM_IN_SESSION_HANDOVER_\",\n130: });\n131: \n132: // The second spelling of a switch, or null when the switch has none.\n133: function switchAlias(key) {\n134:   for (const [prefix, aliasPrefix] of Object.entries(SWITCH_ALIAS_PREFIXES)) {\n135:     if (key.startsWith(prefix)) return aliasPrefix + key.slice(prefix.length);\n136:   }\n137:   return null;\n138: }\n139: \n140: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n141: // 3. HELPERS\n142: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n143: \n144: // The operator config file: sibling of this shared/ dir, in the hooks hub root.\n145: // Overridable via HOOK_FLAGS_CONFIG so tests and alternate layouts can point\n146: // elsewhere without touching the repo copy. Resolved per read (not at load) so\n147: // a late override is honored after _resetConfigCache().\n148: function configPath() {\n149:   return process.env.HOOK_FLAGS_CONFIG || path.join(__dirname, \"..\", \"hook-flags.env\");\n150: }\n151: \n152: const EMPTY_CONFIG = Object.freeze({});\n153: \n154: function isTruthy(value) {\n155:   if (typeof value !== \"string\") return false;\n156:   const v = value.trim().toLowerCase();\n157:   return v === \"1\" || v === \"true\" || v === \"yes\" || v === \"on\";\n158: }\n159: \n160: // Parse a KEY=value config file into a plain object. Blank lines and lines\n161: // beginning with '#' are ignored; a '#' after a space or tab ends the value, so\n162: // the example file's lines, which carry a trailing comment, still work once\n163: // uncommented; surrounding single/double quotes on the value are stripped. The\n164: // other readers of this file (hook-flags.sh, sk-doc's validation_switch.py and\n165: // sk-code's check-dist-staleness.sh) parse it the same way. Any read/parse\n166: // failure returns {} so a broken or absent file can never disable a hook the\n167: // operator did not ask to disable (fail-open).\n168: function loadConfigFile(filePath) {\n169:   let raw;\n170:   try {\n171:     raw = fs.readFileSync(filePath, \"utf8\");\n172:   } catch {\n173:     return {};\n174:   }\n175:   const out = {};\n176:   for (const line of raw.split(/\\r?\\n/)) {\n177:     const trimmed = line.trim();\n178:     if (!trimmed || trimmed.startsWith(\"#\")) continue;\n179:     const eq = trimmed.indexOf(\"=\");\n180:     if (eq <= 0) continue;\n181:     const key = trimmed.slice(0, eq).trim();\n182:     if (!key) continue;\n183:     let val = trimmed.slice(eq + 1).replace(/[ \\t]#.*$/, \"\").trim();\n184:     if (\n185:       val.length >= 2 &&\n186:       ((val[0] === '\"' && val[val.length - 1] === '\"') ||\n187:         (val[0] === \"'\" && val[val.length - 1] === \"'\"))\n188:     ) {\n189:       val = val.slice(1, -1);\n190:     }\n191:     out[key] = val;\n192:   }\n193:   return out;\n194: }\n195: \n196: // The config file is read once per process — hooks run on every turn, so a\n197: // re-read per lookup would be wasteful. Tests reset this via _resetConfigCache.\n198: let cachedConfig = null;\n199: function fileConfig() {\n200:   if (cachedConfig === null) cachedConfig = loadConfigFile(configPath());\n201:   return cachedConfig;\n202: }\n203: function _resetConfigCache() {\n204:   cachedConfig = null;\n205: }\n206: \n207: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n208: // 4. CORE LOGIC\n209: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n210: \n211: // True unless the master switch or one of this concern's kill-switches is set.\n212: // A concern in DEFAULT_OFF also needs its enable switch set before it is true.\n213: // `env` is injectable for testing; it defaults to the live process environment.\n214: // When `env` is passed explicitly, the config file is skipped so tests stay\n215: // isolated — unless a `config` object is also injected to exercise the merge.\n216: // The environment always overrides the config file for a given key.\n217: // `options.runtime` names the calling runtime. For a default-off concern, that\n218: // runtime's enable switch decides whenever it resolves to a non-empty value, and\n219: // the global enable switch decides whenever it does. With both blank, a runtime in\n220: // DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES is on and any other is off. The master and kill switches still\n221: // win over all of them. With no runtime, or one outside RUNTIMES, only the global applies.\n222: function isHookEnabled(concern, env, config, options) {\n223:   return hookFlagDecision(concern, env, config, options).enabled;\n224: }\n225: \n226: // The single decision behind isHookEnabled, with the switch that made it, so a caller\n227: // can tell the operator which flag to change. `key` is the switch that decided, or null\n228: // when nothing did and the concern is on by default. `reason` says which rung of the\n229: // precedence answered: \"master\" (the master switch or an alias), \"kill\" (the concern's\n230: // kill switch or a legacy alias), \"runtime\" (the runtime's enable switch, which held a\n231: // value), \"global\" (the global enable switch, unset or set) or \"default\" (a default-on\n232: // concern, or a default-off one on a runtime DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES names). Arguments are\n233: // those of isHookEnabled.\n234: function hookFlagDecision(concern, env, config, options) {\n235:   const hasEnv = env !== undefined && env !== null;\n236:   const source = hasEnv ? env : process.env;\n237:   const cfg =\n238:     config !== undefined && config !== null\n239:       ? config\n240:       : hasEnv\n241:         ? EMPTY_CONFIG\n242:         : fileConfig();\n243:   const resolve = (key) => {\n244:     const fromEnv = source[key];\n245:     return fromEnv !== undefined ? fromEnv : cfg[key];\n246:   };\n247:   const holds = (value) => typeof value === \"string\" && value.trim() !== \"\";\n248:   // A switch's own value when it holds one, else its second spelling's when that holds\n249:   // one, so `key` names whichever switch the operator actually set.\n250:   const resolveSwitch = (key) => {\n251:     const value = resolve(key);\n252:     const alias = holds(value) ? null : switchAlias(key);\n253:     if (alias && holds(resolve(alias))) return { key: alias, value: resolve(alias) };\n254:     return { key, value };\n255:   };\n256:   for (const key of [MASTER_FLAG].concat(MASTER_ALIASES)) {\n257:     if (isTruthy(resolve(key))) return { enabled: false, key, reason: \"master\" };\n258:   }\n259:   const flag = concernFlag(concern);\n260:   if (flag) {\n261:     const kill = resolveSwitch(flag);\n262:     if (isTruthy(kill.value)) return { enabled: false, key: kill.key, reason: \"kill\" };\n263:   }\n264:   for (const key of LEGACY_ALIASES[concern] || []) {\n265:     if (isTruthy(resolve(key))) return { enabled: false, key, reason: \"kill\" };\n266:   }\n267:   if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(DEFAULT_OFF, concern)) {\n268:     const runtimeFlag = runtimeEnableFlag(concern, options && options.runtime);\n269:     if (runtimeFlag) {\n270:       const runtimeSwitch = resolveSwitch(runtimeFlag);\n271:       if (holds(runtimeSwitch.value)) {\n272:         return { enabled: isTruthy(runtimeSwitch.value), key: runtimeSwitch.key, reason: \"runtime\" };\n273:       }\n274:     }\n275:     const globalSwitch = resolveSwitch(DEFAULT_OFF[concern]);\n276:     if (!holds(globalSwitch.value)) {\n277:       const runtime = options && typeof options.runtime === \"string\" ? options.runtime.trim().toLowerCase() : \"\";\n278:       if ((DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES[concern] || []).includes(runtime)) {\n279:         return { enabled: true, key: null, reason: \"default\" };\n280:       }\n281:     }\n282:     return { enabled: isTruthy(globalSwitch.value), key: globalSwitch.key, reason: \"global\" };\n283:   }\n284:   return { enabled: true, key: null, reason: \"default\" };\n285: }\n286: \n287: // The per-runtime enable switch for a default-off concern, such as\n288: // SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_CLAUDE_ENABLED, or null when the concern has no enable\n289: // switch or the runtime is not one of RUNTIMES.\n290: function runtimeEnableFlag(concern, runtime) {\n291:   if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(DEFAULT_OFF, concern)) return null;\n292:   if (typeof runtime !== \"string\") return null;\n293:   const name = runtime.trim().toLowerCase();\n294:   if (!RUNTIMES.includes(name)) return null;\n295:   return DEFAULT_OFF[concern].replace(/_ENABLED$/, \"_\" + name.toUpperCase() + \"_ENABLED\");\n296: }\n297: \n298: // True when one named switch resolves truthy, for a switch that is not a hook\n299: // concern (a validator's opt-out, say) but is saved in the same file under the\n300: // same precedence: the environment answers whenever the name is set there, even\n301: // to an empty value, and the config file answers otherwise. `env` and `config`\n302: // are injectable exactly as they are for isHookEnabled.\n303: function isFlagOn(name, env, config) {\n304:   if (typeof name !== \"string\" || !name.trim()) return false;\n305:   const hasEnv = env !== undefined && env !== null;\n306:   const source = hasEnv ? env : process.env;\n307:   if (source[name] !== undefined) return isTruthy(source[name]);\n308:   const cfg =\n309:     config !== undefined && config !== null\n310:       ? config\n311:       : hasEnv\n312:         ? EMPTY_CONFIG\n313:         : fileConfig();\n314:   return isTruthy(cfg[name]);\n315: }\n316: \n317: // The canonical per-concern kill-switch env var for a concern slug. A blank\n318: // concern has no flag (skipped), so callers never look up \"MK__DISABLED\".\n319: function concernFlag(concern) {\n320:   if (concern === null || concern === undefined) return null;\n321:   const slug = String(concern).trim();\n322:   if (!slug) return null;\n323:   if (CONCERN_CANONICAL[slug]) return CONCERN_CANONICAL[slug];\n324:   return \"SYSTEM_\" + slug.toUpperCase().replace(/[^A-Z0-9]+/g, \"_\") + \"_DISABLED\";\n325: }\n326: \n327: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n328: // 5. EXPORTS\n329: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n330: \n331: module.exports = {\n332:   isHookEnabled,\n333:   hookFlagDecision,\n334:   isFlagOn,\n335:   concernFlag,\n336:   runtimeEnableFlag,\n337:   isTruthy,\n338:   loadConfigFile,\n339:   MASTER_FLAG,\n340:   MASTER_ALIASES,\n341:   CONCERN_CANONICAL,\n342:   LEGACY_ALIASES,\n343:   DEFAULT_OFF,\n344:   DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES,\n345:   RUNTIMES,\n346:   SWITCH_ALIAS_PREFIXES,\n347:   switchAlias,\n348:   configPath,\n349:   _resetConfigCache,\n350: };","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/hooks/shared/hook-flags.cjs, lines 1-350\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Hook Kill-Switch Resolver\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: \"use strict\";\n5: \n6: // Central kill-switch resolver for every repo-authored runtime hook. A hook is\n7: // enabled by default and only goes silent when the operator sets a kill-switch,\n8: // so adding this guard changes no behavior until a flag is set. The few concerns\n9: // in DEFAULT_OFF invert that and stay silent until their enable switch is set,\n10: // though a kill-switch still silences them. The master\n11: // switch disables the whole enforcement layer at once; a per-concern switch\n12: // disables one hook family across every runtime that carries it. Names that\n13: // predate this convention are honored as aliases so existing operator config\n14: // keeps working.\n15: //\n16: // Flags come from two places: the live environment, and an optional operator\n17: // config file (hook-flags.env) that lets a user persist their choices without\n18: // exporting env vars every session. The environment always wins over the file,\n19: // so a persistent default in the file can still be overridden per session.\n20: \n21: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n22: // 1. IMPORTS\n23: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n24: \n25: const fs = require(\"node:fs\");\n26: const path = require(\"node:path\");\n27: \n28: // Bridge legacy env names onto their new equivalents once, as early as possible.\n29: // Nearly every runtime hook loads this module, so wiring the bridge here covers\n30: // the whole library from a single place; entry points with no flag dependency\n31: // (the standalone daemon launchers) call it themselves.\n32: require(\"./env-aliases.cjs\").applyEnvAliases();\n33: \n34: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n35: // 2. CONSTANTS\n36: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n37: \n38: const MASTER_FLAG = \"SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED\";\n39: // The master switch was renamed from its opaque prefix to a self-describing one.\n40: // The old name stays honored so any operator who exported it keeps their opt-out.\n41: const MASTER_ALIASES = [\"MK_HOOKS_DISABLED\"];\n42: \n43: // Canonical per-concern kill-switch, where the new name does not follow the\n44: // default `SYSTEM_<CONCERN>_DISABLED` shape because the hook is owned by a named\n45: // skill or CLI surface rather than the framework core. Concerns not listed here\n46: // fall back to the default shape in concernFlag().\n47: const CONCERN_CANONICAL = {\n48:   goal: \"OPENCODE_GOAL_DISABLED\",\n49:   dispatch: \"CLI_DISPATCH_AUDIT_DISABLED\",\n50:   \"mcp-route-guard\": \"MCP_ROUTE_GUARD_DISABLED\",\n51:   \"codex-watchdog\": \"CODEX_HOOKS_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\",\n52:   \"git-preflight\": \"SK_GIT_PREFLIGHT_DISABLED\",\n53:   \"post-edit-quality\": \"SK_CODE_POST_EDIT_QUALITY_DISABLED\",\n54: };\n55: \n56: // concern -> extra env vars that also disable it. Three generations coexist here\n57: // so operator config written against any of them keeps working: the still-older\n58: // MK_/SPECKIT_ aliases, and the self-describing variant names that a concern's\n59: // own plugin/config/docs use where they differ from concernFlag()'s canonical\n60: // (e.g. a plugin's *_PLUGIN_DISABLED / *_HOOK_DISABLED const, or a doc's shorter\n61: // spelling). concernFlag() supplies the one canonical name; everything a real\n62: // surface also documents or exports for the same concern is listed here so the\n63: // documented switch actually disables the hook.\n64: const LEGACY_ALIASES = {\n65:   goal: [\"OPENCODE_GOAL_PLUGIN_DISABLED\", \"MK_GOAL_DISABLED\", \"MK_GOAL_PLUGIN_DISABLED\"],\n66:   dispatch: [\"SYSTEM_DISPATCH_DISABLED\", \"MK_DISPATCH_DISABLED\", \"MK_CLI_DISPATCH_AUDIT_DISABLED\"],\n67:   // The preflight guard split off from the audit log, but the whole-surface\n68:   // switch that predates the split still has to silence both halves.\n69:   \"dispatch-guard\": [\"SYSTEM_DISPATCH_DISABLED\", \"MK_DISPATCH_DISABLED\"],\n70:   \"skill-advisor\": [\n71:     \"SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED\",\n72:     \"SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED\",\n73:     \"MK_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED\",\n74:     \"MK_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED\",\n75:     \"MK_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED\",\n76:     \"SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED\",\n77:     \"SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED\",\n78:   ],\n79:   completion: [\n80:     \"SYSTEM_SPECKIT_COMPLETION_DISABLED\",\n81:     \"SYSTEM_COMPLETION_SENTINEL_DISABLED\",\n82:     \"MK_COMPLETION_DISABLED\",\n83:     \"MK_COMPLETION_SENTINEL_DISABLED\",\n84:     \"MK_SPECKIT_COMPLETION_DISABLED\",\n85:   ],\n86:   \"spec-gate\": [\"MK_SPEC_GATE_DISABLED\", \"SPECKIT_SPEC_GATE_DISABLED\"],\n87:   \"mcp-route-guard\": [\"MK_MCP_ROUTE_GUARD_DISABLED\"],\n88:   \"codex-watchdog\": [\"CODEX_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\", \"MK_CODEX_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\", \"MK_CODEX_HOOKS_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\"],\n89:   \"dist-freshness\": [\"MK_DIST_FRESHNESS_DISABLED\", \"MK_DIST_FRESHNESS_GUARD_DISABLED\"],\n90:   \"git-preflight\": [\"MK_GIT_PREFLIGHT_DISABLED\"],\n91:   \"hook-install\": [\"MK_HOOK_INSTALL_DISABLED\"],\n92:   \"permission-policy\": [\"MK_PERMISSION_POLICY_DISABLED\"],\n93:   \"session-lifecycle\": [\"MK_SESSION_LIFECYCLE_DISABLED\"],\n94:   \"session-cleanup\": [\"MK_SESSION_CLEANUP_DISABLED\"],\n95:   \"task-dispatch\": [\"MK_TASK_DISPATCH_DISABLED\"],\n96:   \"post-edit-quality\": [\"MK_POST_EDIT_QUALITY_DISABLED\"],\n97: };\n98: \n99: // concern -> the switch that turns it on. A concern listed here is off until the\n100: // operator opts in, because it acts on the session itself (the relaunch loop can\n101: // end a running session and start a new one), except on a runtime DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES\n102: // names for it. The opt-in resolves like every other flag, environment over file,\n103: // and the master and kill switches still win over it.\n104: const DEFAULT_OFF = {\n105:   \"session-handover\": \"SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_ENABLED\",\n106: };\n107: \n108: // The CLI runtimes that can carry a default-off concern. An operator may want the\n109: // relaunch loop on in one runtime and off in another, so each runtime gets its own\n110: // enable switch, SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_<RUNTIME>_ENABLED, which answers before\n111: // the global one whenever it holds a value. A caller names its runtime explicitly;\n112: // the resolver never guesses it from the environment.\n113: const RUNTIMES = Object.freeze([\"claude\", \"codex\", \"pi\", \"opencode\", \"cursor\", \"devin\", \"vibe\"]);\n114: \n115: // concern -> the runtimes where a default-off concern is on when neither its runtime\n116: // switch nor its global switch holds a value. Claude Code's hand-over needs no extra\n117: // wiring beyond the hooks every install registers, so a project gets it without\n118: // finding the switch; the other runtimes still wait for their owner to opt in. A set\n119: // value, the master switch and the kill switches all answer before this default.\n120: const DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES = Object.freeze({\n121:   \"session-handover\": Object.freeze([\"claude\"]),\n122: });\n123: \n124: // Main switch prefix -> the second spelling also read for it. People call the hand-over\n125: // \"in-session handover\" too, so a switch written with that name works. The second\n126: // spelling answers only when the main switch is unset or blank, so the main name wins\n127: // whenever both hold a value.\n128: const SWITCH_ALIAS_PREFIXES = Object.freeze({\n129:   SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_: \"SYSTEM_IN_SESSION_HANDOVER_\",\n130: });\n131: \n132: // The second spelling of a switch, or null when the switch has none.\n133: function switchAlias(key) {\n134:   for (const [prefix, aliasPrefix] of Object.entries(SWITCH_ALIAS_PREFIXES)) {\n135:     if (key.startsWith(prefix)) return aliasPrefix + key.slice(prefix.length);\n136:   }\n137:   return null;\n138: }\n139: \n140: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n141: // 3. HELPERS\n142: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n143: \n144: // The operator config file: sibling of this shared/ dir, in the hooks hub root.\n145: // Overridable via HOOK_FLAGS_CONFIG so tests and alternate layouts can point\n146: // elsewhere without touching the repo copy. Resolved per read (not at load) so\n147: // a late override is honored after _resetConfigCache().\n148: function configPath() {\n149:   return process.env.HOOK_FLAGS_CONFIG || path.join(__dirname, \"..\", \"hook-flags.env\");\n150: }\n151: \n152: const EMPTY_CONFIG = Object.freeze({});\n153: \n154: function isTruthy(value) {\n155:   if (typeof value !== \"string\") return false;\n156:   const v = value.trim().toLowerCase();\n157:   return v === \"1\" || v === \"true\" || v === \"yes\" || v === \"on\";\n158: }\n159: \n160: // Parse a KEY=value config file into a plain object. Blank lines and lines\n161: // beginning with '#' are ignored; a '#' after a space or tab ends the value, so\n162: // the example file's lines, which carry a trailing comment, still work once\n163: // uncommented; surrounding single/double quotes on the value are stripped. The\n164: // other readers of this file (hook-flags.sh, sk-doc's validation_switch.py and\n165: // sk-code's check-dist-staleness.sh) parse it the same way. Any read/parse\n166: // failure returns {} so a broken or absent file can never disable a hook the\n167: // operator did not ask to disable (fail-open).\n168: function loadConfigFile(filePath) {\n169:   let raw;\n170:   try {\n171:     raw = fs.readFileSync(filePath, \"utf8\");\n172:   } catch {\n173:     return {};\n174:   }\n175:   const out = {};\n176:   for (const line of raw.split(/\\r?\\n/)) {\n177:     const trimmed = line.trim();\n178:     if (!trimmed || trimmed.startsWith(\"#\")) continue;\n179:     const eq = trimmed.indexOf(\"=\");\n180:     if (eq <= 0) continue;\n181:     const key = trimmed.slice(0, eq).trim();\n182:     if (!key) continue;\n183:     let val = trimmed.slice(eq + 1).replace(/[ \\t]#.*$/, \"\").trim();\n184:     if (\n185:       val.length >= 2 &&\n186:       ((val[0] === '\"' && val[val.length - 1] === '\"') ||\n187:         (val[0] === \"'\" && val[val.length - 1] === \"'\"))\n188:     ) {\n189:       val = val.slice(1, -1);\n190:     }\n191:     out[key] = val;\n192:   }\n193:   return out;\n194: }\n195: \n196: // The config file is read once per process — hooks run on every turn, so a\n197: // re-read per lookup would be wasteful. Tests reset this via _resetConfigCache.\n198: let cachedConfig = null;\n199: function fileConfig() {\n200:   if (cachedConfig === null) cachedConfig = loadConfigFile(configPath());\n201:   return cachedConfig;\n202: }\n203: function _resetConfigCache() {\n204:   cachedConfig = null;\n205: }\n206: \n207: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n208: // 4. CORE LOGIC\n209: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n210: \n211: // True unless the master switch or one of this concern's kill-switches is set.\n212: // A concern in DEFAULT_OFF also needs its enable switch set before it is true.\n213: // `env` is injectable for testing; it defaults to the live process environment.\n214: // When `env` is passed explicitly, the config file is skipped so tests stay\n215: // isolated — unless a `config` object is also injected to exercise the merge.\n216: // The environment always overrides the config file for a given key.\n217: // `options.runtime` names the calling runtime. For a default-off concern, that\n218: // runtime's enable switch decides whenever it resolves to a non-empty value, and\n219: // the global enable switch decides whenever it does. With both blank, a runtime in\n220: // DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES is on and any other is off. The master and kill switches still\n221: // win over all of them. With no runtime, or one outside RUNTIMES, only the global applies.\n222: function isHookEnabled(concern, env, config, options) {\n223:   return hookFlagDecision(concern, env, config, options).enabled;\n224: }\n225: \n226: // The single decision behind isHookEnabled, with the switch that made it, so a caller\n227: // can tell the operator which flag to change. `key` is the switch that decided, or null\n228: // when nothing did and the concern is on by default. `reason` says which rung of the\n229: // precedence answered: \"master\" (the master switch or an alias), \"kill\" (the concern's\n230: // kill switch or a legacy alias), \"runtime\" (the runtime's enable switch, which held a\n231: // value), \"global\" (the global enable switch, unset or set) or \"default\" (a default-on\n232: // concern, or a default-off one on a runtime DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES names). Arguments are\n233: // those of isHookEnabled.\n234: function hookFlagDecision(concern, env, config, options) {\n235:   const hasEnv = env !== undefined && env !== null;\n236:   const source = hasEnv ? env : process.env;\n237:   const cfg =\n238:     config !== undefined && config !== null\n239:       ? config\n240:       : hasEnv\n241:         ? EMPTY_CONFIG\n242:         : fileConfig();\n243:   const resolve = (key) => {\n244:     const fromEnv = source[key];\n245:     return fromEnv !== undefined ? fromEnv : cfg[key];\n246:   };\n247:   const holds = (value) => typeof value === \"string\" && value.trim() !== \"\";\n248:   // A switch's own value when it holds one, else its second spelling's when that holds\n249:   // one, so `key` names whichever switch the operator actually set.\n250:   const resolveSwitch = (key) => {\n251:     const value = resolve(key);\n252:     const alias = holds(value) ? null : switchAlias(key);\n253:     if (alias && holds(resolve(alias))) return { key: alias, value: resolve(alias) };\n254:     return { key, value };\n255:   };\n256:   for (const key of [MASTER_FLAG].concat(MASTER_ALIASES)) {\n257:     if (isTruthy(resolve(key))) return { enabled: false, key, reason: \"master\" };\n258:   }\n259:   const flag = concernFlag(concern);\n260:   if (flag) {\n261:     const kill = resolveSwitch(flag);\n262:     if (isTruthy(kill.value)) return { enabled: false, key: kill.key, reason: \"kill\" };\n263:   }\n264:   for (const key of LEGACY_ALIASES[concern] || []) {\n265:     if (isTruthy(resolve(key))) return { enabled: false, key, reason: \"kill\" };\n266:   }\n267:   if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(DEFAULT_OFF, concern)) {\n268:     const runtimeFlag = runtimeEnableFlag(concern, options && options.runtime);\n269:     if (runtimeFlag) {\n270:       const runtimeSwitch = resolveSwitch(runtimeFlag);\n271:       if (holds(runtimeSwitch.value)) {\n272:         return { enabled: isTruthy(runtimeSwitch.value), key: runtimeSwitch.key, reason: \"runtime\" };\n273:       }\n274:     }\n275:     const globalSwitch = resolveSwitch(DEFAULT_OFF[concern]);\n276:     if (!holds(globalSwitch.value)) {\n277:       const runtime = options && typeof options.runtime === \"string\" ? options.runtime.trim().toLowerCase() : \"\";\n278:       if ((DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES[concern] || []).includes(runtime)) {\n279:         return { enabled: true, key: null, reason: \"default\" };\n280:       }\n281:     }\n282:     return { enabled: isTruthy(globalSwitch.value), key: globalSwitch.key, reason: \"global\" };\n283:   }\n284:   return { enabled: true, key: null, reason: \"default\" };\n285: }\n286: \n287: // The per-runtime enable switch for a default-off concern, such as\n288: // SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_CLAUDE_ENABLED, or null when the concern has no enable\n289: // switch or the runtime is not one of RUNTIMES.\n290: function runtimeEnableFlag(concern, runtime) {\n291:   if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(DEFAULT_OFF, concern)) return null;\n292:   if (typeof runtime !== \"string\") return null;\n293:   const name = runtime.trim().toLowerCase();\n294:   if (!RUNTIMES.includes(name)) return null;\n295:   return DEFAULT_OFF[concern].replace(/_ENABLED$/, \"_\" + name.toUpperCase() + \"_ENABLED\");\n296: }\n297: \n298: // True when one named switch resolves truthy, for a switch that is not a hook\n299: // concern (a validator's opt-out, say) but is saved in the same file under the\n300: // same precedence: the environment answers whenever the name is set there, even\n301: // to an empty value, and the config file answers otherwise. `env` and `config`\n302: // are injectable exactly as they are for isHookEnabled.\n303: function isFlagOn(name, env, config) {\n304:   if (typeof name !== \"string\" || !name.trim()) return false;\n305:   const hasEnv = env !== undefined && env !== null;\n306:   const source = hasEnv ? env : process.env;\n307:   if (source[name] !== undefined) return isTruthy(source[name]);\n308:   const cfg =\n309:     config !== undefined && config !== null\n310:       ? config\n311:       : hasEnv\n312:         ? EMPTY_CONFIG\n313:         : fileConfig();\n314:   return isTruthy(cfg[name]);\n315: }\n316: \n317: // The canonical per-concern kill-switch env var for a concern slug. A blank\n318: // concern has no flag (skipped), so callers never look up \"MK__DISABLED\".\n319: function concernFlag(concern) {\n320:   if (concern === null || concern === undefined) return null;\n321:   const slug = String(concern).trim();\n322:   if (!slug) return null;\n323:   if (CONCERN_CANONICAL[slug]) return CONCERN_CANONICAL[slug];\n324:   return \"SYSTEM_\" + slug.toUpperCase().replace(/[^A-Z0-9]+/g, \"_\") + \"_DISABLED\";\n325: }\n326: \n327: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n328: // 5. EXPORTS\n329: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n330: \n331: module.exports = {\n332:   isHookEnabled,\n333:   hookFlagDecision,\n334:   isFlagOn,\n335:   concernFlag,\n336:   runtimeEnableFlag,\n337:   isTruthy,\n338:   loadConfigFile,\n339:   MASTER_FLAG,\n340:   MASTER_ALIASES,\n341:   CONCERN_CANONICAL,\n342:   LEGACY_ALIASES,\n343:   DEFAULT_OFF,\n344:   DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES,\n345:   RUNTIMES,\n346:   SWITCH_ALIAS_PREFIXES,\n347:   switchAlias,\n348:   configPath,\n349:   _resetConfigCache,\n350: };"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/hooks/shared/hook-flags.cjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794760661701,"end":1794760662093}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794760663350,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd5ffeS4J0KdMtC6TlEK","part":{"id":"prt_1e0126136001dfnotRF8GrEWJW","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd5ffeS4J0KdMtC6TlEK","messageID":"msg_1e0124d80001Av7zN3Q7EvfRXj","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"50a86b40bc8d1f4b808369abe9284d0ec57df732","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":4923,"output":71,"reasoning":83,"cache":{"read":79744,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794760666715,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd5ffeS4J0KdMtC6TlEK","part":{"id":"prt_1e0126e5b001w7PnjkS3g0O19p","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd5ffeS4J0KdMtC6TlEK","messageID":"msg_1e012613b0018Gu4wyXBIJ2yar","type":"step-start","snapshot":"50a86b40bc8d1f4b808369abe9284d0ec57df732"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794760675808,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd5ffeS4J0KdMtC6TlEK","part":{"partID":"prt_1e0127ee1001iiK4XadwEFf46y","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd5ffeS4J0KdMtC6TlEK","messageID":"msg_1e012613b0018Gu4wyXBIJ2yar","type":"tool","id":"call_b550c1a4a30a496eaf28412e","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && grep -rn \"disabledRecommendOutput\\|advisorDisabledBy\\|ADVISOR_DISABLED_FLAGS\\|ADVISOR_DISABLED\\|SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED\\|SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED\" --include=*.ts --include=*.cjs --include=*.mjs --include=*.js --include=*.py --include=*.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor .skilled/bin .skilled/hooks .skilled/commands | grep -v node_modules | head -80"},"output":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/INSTALL-GUIDE.md:52:- `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` is unset unless you are intentionally testing rollback.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/INSTALL-GUIDE.md:174:| `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` | Disables prompt-time advisor briefs and makes the CLI and the scorer skip advisor work. |\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/INSTALL-GUIDE.md:181:export SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/INSTALL-GUIDE.md:193:unset SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md:24:`hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` reads the prompt from stdin, calls the native advisor and returns a JSON envelope with `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`. The hook honors `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` and fails open on any daemon-level failure. As a default-off, fail-open shadow it also records post-emission advisor-policy delivery (`observeEmittedAdvisorPolicy`), an observed receipt with `lifecycleEpoch >= 1`, as the final step before returning, leaving the emitted envelope byte-identical. Freshness vocabulary is `live / stale / absent / unavailable`. Status vocabulary is `ok / skipped / degraded / fail_open`.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/opencode-plugin-bridge.md:24:`.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js` exports the plugin. It spawns `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs` for each advisor call, bounded at a 64 KiB stdin request, a 256 KiB stdout cap and a 2 KiB brief cap. The request carries at most the first 10,000 prompt characters, the most `advisor_recommend` accepts. A budget too small for any prompt spawns nothing. Cache TTL is 5 minutes. The live threshold pair is `confidenceThreshold: 0.8` and `uncertaintyThreshold: 0.35`. The CLI call times out after 2500 ms with SIGKILL escalation. Opt-out via `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` or `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED=1` (with legacy `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` and `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED=1` aliases) or `enabled: false` in plugin config. The plugin renders through the shared `renderAdvisorBrief` contract loaded from the compiled package instead of pinning private handler paths. A degraded CLI answer, returned when the daemon is unreachable, is labeled `route: \"cli-local-scorer\"` and otherwise renders like any other recommendation.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md:23:`handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` implements the command. Input is validated by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` (Zod strict). The public response always includes the resolved `workspaceRoot` plus `effectiveThresholds`, where `effectiveThresholds` publishes the active `confidenceThreshold`, `uncertaintyThreshold` and `confidenceOnly` mode after request overrides are merged with defaults. Output also carries prompt-safe `recommendations[]`, optional `laneBreakdown[]` when `includeAttribution: true`, lifecycle redirect metadata (`redirectFrom`, `redirectTo`, `status`), `freshness`, `trustState`, `generatedAt`, `cache`, optional `warnings` and optional `abstainReasons`. Fail-open states such as disabled or absent freshness still preserve `workspaceRoot` and `effectiveThresholds` in the envelope so callers can inspect the resolved repository scope and active routing thresholds even when no recommendations are returned. The disabled envelope is built by `lib/advisor-disabled.ts`, which the handler and the CLI client both call. The client calls it first, in the caller's own process, so the direct call abstains whenever the skill-advisor hooks are off, by the same rule the adapters use: the canonical `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED`, its aliases such as `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` and `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED`, the master switch `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED`, any truthy value or a line in the flags file. It abstains before it reaches a daemon and before the local scorer runs. A daemon that does answer reads only its own environment. The handler enforces sanitization at the envelope boundary (see `lib/derived/sanitizer.ts`) and never echoes prompt text into response metadata. Cache behavior is tied to generation (see [`daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md`](../../feature-catalog/daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md)).\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/python-compat/cli-shim.md:30:| `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` | Disable advisor invocation entirely. |\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/changelog/v1.1.0.0.md:73:A direct `advisor_recommend` call now abstains whenever the skill-advisor hooks are off. It decides in the caller's own process, under `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED`, its aliases, the master switch or the flags file. That happens before any daemon starts or the scorer runs. The launcher passes no switch name to a daemon it starts.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/references/decisions/deferred-decisions.md:81:- The disable-flag interaction (per `INSTALL-GUIDE.md` §8): Devin hook checks `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` first plus falls back to `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED`. Migration does not change this contract.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/stress-test/skill-advisor/python-compat-stress.vitest.ts:95:        SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED: '1',\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/stress-test/skill-advisor/python-compat-stress.vitest.ts:104:        SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED: '1',\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/stress-test/skill-advisor/python-compat-stress.vitest.ts:113:      reason: 'ADVISOR_DISABLED',\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/stress-test/skill-advisor/opencode-plugin-bridge-stress.vitest.ts:82:      'SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED',\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/stress-test/skill-advisor/opencode-plugin-bridge-stress.vitest.ts:87:    delete process.env.SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED;\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/runtime/skill-advisor-cli.js:16:import { disabledRecommendOutput } from './lib/advisor-disabled.js';\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/runtime/skill-advisor-cli.js:1375:    const disabled = disabledRecommendOutput(args);\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/runtime/lib/advisor-disabled.js:44:export const ADVISOR_DISABLED_FLAGS = hookFlags === null\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/runtime/lib/advisor-disabled.js:54:export function advisorDisabledBy(env = process.env, config) {\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/runtime/lib/advisor-disabled.js:106:export function disabledRecommendOutput(input, env = process.env, config) {\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/runtime/lib/advisor-disabled.js:107:    const disabledBy = advisorDisabledBy(env, config);\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/runtime/lib/advisor-disabled.js:126:        trustState: unavailableTrustState('ADVISOR_DISABLED'),\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/runtime/lib/advisor-disabled.js:127: